After doing a lot of digging / research, here's where I'm at for how to implement this (and what I'm still trying to figure out).

I'm not sure if trying to stick to (pseudo) C# syntax is helpful or not, but I'll continue.

Assume we want to encode something like:
```
public interface ISelectable< T<?> > /* a.k.a. "Functor" */
{ 
    // Aka `map` or `fmap`
    T<B> Select<A, B>(T<A> ta, Func<A, B> mapFunc);
}
```

What if `T<a1, a2, ..., a_n>` was encoded as `GenericParam`, such that valid instantiations had to be sub-types of the type `System.IGeneric'n<...>`, where all generic classes/interfaces now either extended/implemented `System.IGeneric'n<...>` or extended classes/interfaces which themselves extended/implemented `System.IGeneric'n<...>`?


Kind of "compiler-generated" code:
```
// Here, we've encoded that the `TC` type parameter has a unary type-constructor (kind: "* -> *")
public interface ISelectable< TC > where TC : System.IGeneric`1<System.__Canon>>
{
    // We'll get to the `Select` function later...
}
```

The "canonical instantiation" of (e.g.) "List\<T\>" is `List<System.__Canon>`, which necessarily
would implement (hence be a sub-type of) `System.IGeneric'1<System.__Canon>`, so this would be allowed:

```
public struct ListSelectable : ISelectable<List<?>> { /* ... */ }

// ...or if you prefer the syntax...

public struct ListSelectable : ISelectable<List<>> { /* ... */ }
```

But these would be illegal:

```
// Illegal Example 1:
public struct Bad1<A> : ISelectable<List<A>> { /* ... */ } 

// Illegal Example 2:
public struct Bad2 : ISelectable<List<string>> { /* ... */ } 
```

Higher-order type operators (beyond simple unary type-constructors with kind "* -> *") could be encoded like (again kind of "compiler-generated" pseudo-code):
```
// public interface Whatever< K< G<?, ?> > >
// `G` has kind: * -> * -> *
// `K` has kind: (* -> * -> *) -> *

public interface Whatever< K, G >
    where G : System.IGeneric`2<System.__Canon, System.__Canon>>
    where K : System.IGeneric`1<G>
{
    // ...
}
```

This would impose no limitations on using higher-kinded type arguments with classes, we just use 
the canonical instantiation of the `TC` type argument, for whatever `IGeneric'n<...>` type is specified.
```
class Test<F<>> { }

// ...later...

var a = new Test<List<>>();  // The type argument for `TC` := `F<>` here will be `List<System.__Canon>`
```


**CLR changes / encoding ideas**:
    
-   `System.__Canon` is our "hole type", as in `List<?>` (or `List<>` if you prefer), and for some specific type-constructor, it _is_ a proper type (to reiterate, it is the canonical instantiation of that generic type, namely `List<System.__Canon>`)


-   `TC` (from the first example) could be encoded with no changes to `GenericParam` (ECMA-335: II.22.20) and would require no new metadata tables, we instead need a new "Flag for Generic Parameter" (ECMA-335: II.23.1.7), and a "GenericParamConstraint" (ECMA-335: II.22.21), for the appropriate `IGeneric'n<...>`


-   Upper-bounds for type-constructor type parameters should be possible by simply changing the "GenericParamConstraint", (e.g. using `System.Collection.IEnumerable<System.__Canon>` instead of `System.IGeneric'1<System.__Canon>`), which could be expressed similar to today (i.e. `class Test<F<>> where F<> : IEnumerable<> {}`)


**Need to figure out / problems**:

-    Partial application seems possible, i.e. "Dictionary<,>" subtypes `System.IGeneric'2<__Canon,__Canon>`, but (if you'll allow the "loose" syntax) somehow "Dictionary<int, ?>" needs to subtype `System.IGeneric'1<__Canon>` (as well as `System.IGeneric'2<int, __Canon>`) which would seem to imply that:
         > System.IGeneric'2\<A, B\> : System.IGeneric'1\<A\>, System.IGeneric'1\<B\>
         > System.IGeneric'3\<A, B, C\> : System.IGeneric'2\<A, B\>, System.IGeneric'2\<A, C\>, System.IGeneric'2\<B, C\>
         > ...and so on...
      but I'm just not sure this is correct. It _seems_ like it will work, but I haven't worked it out enough to be sure this doesn't allow some unsound typing. Also the permutations for higher arity type-constructors will be "_large_" - I don't know if the CLR has issues with a "large" number of interfaces (for some definition of "large")?


-    How to encode variance into higher-kinded type parameters?

-    Type application of `T` to some (e.g.) `F<>` - in the context of a class/interface/method were unary type constructor (or higher-order) `F<>` has been defined, we know that applying arity-`n type arguments to that type constructor yields a proper type (or for higher-order, yields another type-constructor) ...
      - Do we implement this by extending method dispatch / JIT code generation? 
      - Do we _still_ need "generic generics"? (e.g. generic arguments can be owned by generic arguments, or something equivalent)
      - Does this affect code sharing for generics? Performance?
      - A CLR-internal "Kleisli" like data structure looks interesting, which would have some CLR level "ApplyType(...)" function, but...
        - ...then do we need another metadata table to define and assign ownership of "Kleislis" for a given type/class (it looks like it / probably)?
        - ...or is the JIT robust enough that we don't need to pre-generate these at instantiation?
        - ...or do we need to do this anyway for performance reasons?
    

-    And finally (probably most applicable to C#, before Shapes/Concepts are implemented), how do we handle inheritance-based implementation, given that we don't have type-classes in C#? As a simple example, consider: 
```
    public interface ISelectable< T<?> > /* a.k.a. "Functor", from before */
    { 
        T<B> Select<A, B>(T<A> ta, Func<A, B> mapFunc);
    }

    // This doesn't work / isn't right, because obviously `A` cannot be `System.__Canon`    
    public class MyCollection<A> : ISelectable<MyCollection<A>>
    {
        /// But also how do we indicate that this implements / has the same signature as
        //`Select` from `ISelectable`?
        public MyCollection<B> Select<B>(MyCollection<A> ta, Func<A, B> mapFunc) { throw new NotImplementedException(); }

        // ...and this isn't right either because of the ambiguity/re-introduction of `A`, which isn't a new type variable.
        public MyCollection<B> Select<A, B>(MyCollection<A> ta, Func<A, B> mapFunc) { throw new NotImplementedException(); }

    }
```

There are solutions with code generation (compiler?), and there might be some solution with some sort of template and F-bounds, but I'd really rather not / it looks disgusting!

This isn't a problem if (for C#) class/interface level generics cannot be higher-kinded, and these only appear in functions/methods, but then you lose a lot of the expressiveness I think people want from this feature.

**Is there anything here that is missing or is known to be incorrect?**

