{
    // This is now fine because after substitution of MyCollection for T in ISelectable the types here match up
    public MyCollection<B> Select<B>(MyCollection<A> ta, Func<A, B> mapFunc) { throw new NotImplementedException(); }
```

The syntax `ISelectable<X => T<X>>` has an encoding at the CLR that ISelectable has a generic param T, and that T has a generic param X.

I think even compose is doable if you separate constructors from types:
```scala
  def compose[G[_]: Functor]: Functor[λ[α => F[G[α]]]] =
    new ComposedFunctor[F, G] {
      val F = self
      val G = Functor[G]
    }
```
would become something like the following in C#, it looks a bit different because instance interfaces not traits but it's still functor composition, and you could rewrite it to look more trait like.
```csharp
class ComposedFunctor<X => F<X>, X => G<X>, T> : IFunctor<X => F<G<X>>> where F : IFunctor<>, G : IFunctor<> {

    public ComposedFunctor(F<G<T>> value) { this.Value = value; }

    public ComposedFunctor<F, G, U> Map<U>(func : Func<T,U>) {
        return value.Map(g => g.Map(func))
    }
}
```

I'm using `IFunctor<>` as short hand for `IFunctor<X => T<X>>` i.e. use the generic params it already has it.

