Well now this is confusing because not `F<A>` has different meaning if it's lsited in generics or is an actual type. Let's see your original example:

```cs
F<A> Foo<F<A>, F<B>>(F<A> fa, F<B> fb)=> fa;
//^       ^
//|       +------------ here it means * -> *
//+-------------------- but here it means *
```

As you can see it's ambiguous. And if it's ambiguous it is for humans reading the code: you need to always track context to make sure you're reading this right.

I don't think it's a good way to go.

